                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "237653901",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198025103263",
                            "num_reviews": 5,
                            "playtime_forever": 25,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 25,
                            "deck_playtime_at_review": 25,
                            "last_played": 1791860757
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "If you want random bits of funny/quirky text and pixel art heavily laden with references, you might like the Orion Trail experience. I recommend coming at it as if it were a Choose Your Own Adventure book, and completely ignore any implication that you have any kind of agency. Perhaps this was ahead of its time and predicted the shortform content boom of the late 2010s, and will appeal to fans of shortform video scrolling.\n\nI was really hoping for a humorous Oregon Trail In Space clone, especially after enjoying the heck out of Organ Trail, and this comes close -- but it's in an uncanny valley where it looks like it will be enjoyable but it's like having a conversation with someone who has no short term memory and never blinks. As the description says \"only the luckiest, bravest, and most stubborn captains will make it all the way to the end.\"\n\nThe reason that I requested a refund for this game was because of my frustration that it gave the appearance of choice, narrative, and usability, and has none of those. Clearly a lot of effort was put into the creation of the text and art assets, it's unfortunate that it has so many frustration points.\n\nMy main gripes, having played for less than an hour:\n\n1) Your run is not saved. Exit the game for any reason and you have to start a new run.\n\n2) You can choose and name your crew but it doesn't matter, events are random and meaningless. In one run my first encounter involved being chased down by the authorities and court-martialed. I failed my attempt to \"present evidence\" or something which was an argument that whatever we were being told we had done (recall this was the first encounter) wasn't actually a bad thing; critical-failed the attempt, and was then told that we would be on the run from the space police from then on. This had no effect or consequence other than reducing one of the four numbers at the top of the screen.\n\n3) Buttons, images, numbers -- things are clickable, you can click on a purple planet or a green planet, but choices don't matter and the buttons don't do interesting \"toy-like\" things (see: the excellent book on game design by Jesse Schell), other than roulette which gets old real fast. Which is more frustrating to me than if there was one button that said \"click me and I'll generate a funny image/text combination!\"\n\nIf you have played and enjoyed The Oregon Trail, Organ Trail, FTL, or even Fighting Fantasy or one of the other stat-tracking CYOA books, and are hoping for a remotely similar experience in any way, this isn't it.",
